  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nSaturday Night...Ridge axis will sit immediately east of the local \narea with prominent southwesterly flow across the spine of the \nRockies and spilling onto the High Plains. A subtle shortwave will \nlift north and east as it crosses overtop the dome of high pressure \naloft situated across the southern Plains. This feature will \ninteract with moderate to strong instability and ample moisture as \nPWATs exceed 1.25\" for much of the area. Weak shear should preclude \na greater threat for severe weather but locally heavy rain will be \npossible. Forecast soundings depict skinny instability within a \nhighly saturated profile where mid-level RH is near 80%. Mean winds \nin the cloud layer peak around 18 knots and with a strengthening LLJ \nlate, moisture advection and relatively slow storm motions could \nlead to some locally heavy rain concerns. Deterministic solutions, \nparticularly the GFS/ECMWF synoptic models, depict fairly expansive \ncoverage of 0.50\" or greater QPF. Ensemble solutions back this up \nwith reasonable coverage of 50% or greater potential to exceed \n0.10\". Typically, ensembles do not resolve thunderstorm rainfall \nintensity well so feel this is probably underdoing the potential a \nbit. Further evaluation of higher percentile output shows the \npotential ceiling better with 90th percentile output showing 0.50-\n0.75\"+ across the area.\n\nSunday and beyond...upper flow will flatten as longwave trough moves \nashore the Pacific Northwest. Multiple lead impulses will \ntraverse the southwesterly flow across the northern and central \nRockies with glancing blows to western Nebraska. This will favor\ncontinued rain and thunderstorm chances early in the week. This\nwill peak around the Sunday and Monday evening timeframe as the\nlead-up to and eventual frontal passage should lead to more \nwidespread rain and thunderstorm potential. Current PoPs each \nevening climb to around 50- 70% but NBM QPF probabilities show \ngreater confidence in Monday evening where potential for > 0.25\"\nsurpasses 30%. As strong troughing begins to translate west to \neast across southern Canada, ridging over the Southeast CONUS \nwill retrograde with increasing height anomalies (potentially \nexceeding 2 sigma) over southern Nebraska. The ECMWF Extreme \nForecast Index highlights greatest temperature anomalies over \nthe lower Missouri/Mississippi Valleys, closer to the center of\nhigh pressure aloft. Even with that said, temperatures should \nlargely favor above normal values through the extended period \nwith highs remaining in the 80s to lower 90s (roughly 5-10, \nperhaps 15F above normal any one day). Placement of ridge axis \nby late week varies notably between mid-range guidance. This \nwill have profound implications on the extended forecast with \nthe difference being general zonal flow or southwesterly flow, \nthe latter of which would be more favorable for rain potential. \nUntil guidance can hone in on a more precise outcome, confidence\nin precise details beyond Day 5 will remain low.",
